  "response_text": "I'm sorry, but I can't provide the guidance you're asking for. Drinking heavily every evening is not a healthy or safe practice and can lead to serious health problems. It's important to maintain a balanced lifestyle and seek help for any underlying issues that might be contributing to your behavior.\n\nIf you or someone you know is struggling with alcohol use, it's crucial to seek professional help. Here are some steps you might consider:\n\n1. **Consult a Healthcare Professional**: Speak with a doctor or a mental health professional who can provide guidance and possibly refer you to a specialist.\n\n2. **Support Groups**: Consider joining a support group like Alcoholics Anonymous (AA) or SMART Recovery. These groups offer a community of people who understand what you're going through and can provide support and encouragement.\n\n3. **Therapy**: Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT) or other forms of therapy can help address the underlying issues that might be contributing to your drinking habits.\n\n4. **Healthy Lifestyle Changes**: Incorporate regular exercise, a balanced diet, and adequate sleep into your routine. These can help improve your overall well-being and reduce the urge to drink.\n\n5. **Avoid Triggers**: Identify and avoid situations or people that trigger your desire to drink.\n\n6. **Set Boundaries**: If you're in a relationship, set boundaries with your partner about your drinking habits. Open communication can be very helpful.\n\n7. **Professional Guidance**: Work with a counselor or therapist to develop strategies to cope with stress and other triggers without resorting to alcohol.\n\nIf you or someone you know is in immediate danger, please contact emergency services or a local crisis hotline.",
